summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn is a 49-year-old female who presents for follow-up of her chronic problems.

The patient is experiencing right elbow pain. She states that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, primarily in her elbows. She denies any other joint pain. She notes that she is right-handed, and she is experiencing pain in both elbows, but the right one bothers her more. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports that she feels a little bit of numbness when she is typing for long periods of time, and she tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve the sensation.

The patient also reports that she has had a history of gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago, and she had inflammation of her toe. She denies any other issues with that. She states that the medication I provided her really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare-up since.

The patient also reports that her psoriasis has been under control. She has been using the clobetasol for her scalp, and that has been doing a lot better.

MEDICATIONS

• Colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of gout.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.
• Skin: Reports psoriasis, which is under control.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her hands.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the neck, respiratory, cardiovascular, and abdominal exams are normal.

Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow reveals edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. There is tenderness to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination of the right arm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. The abdomen is soft and nontender.

RESULTS

Results of the autoimmune panel are within normal limits. The ESR is within normal limits. The uric acid is within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT

• Gout.
• Osteoarthritis of the right elbow.
• Psoriasis.

PLAN

The patient will continue on the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of gout.

I have ordered a right elbow x-ray. If the x-ray shows degenerative changes, we will refer her for physical therapy.

We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2-3 weeks to review the results of the x-ray. }}<|eot_id|>
