summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain.

The patient reports that she was at a birthday party and it was a surprise party. She states that they were all like crunched behind and hiding and when the person came in they all jumped out and then she fell down the little stair and she rammed her back into the railing of the stairs. The patient states that it hurt when she jumped up and maybe twisting. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She states that it is more on the right side. She states that if she is lying flat it hurts a little more and any twisting motion for sure. She denies any numbness or tingling. She states that she had a lumbar fusion. She reports that she doesn't have any back problems before.

The patient reports that she was given ibuprofen for her symptoms.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has a history of anxiety and she takes medication for it.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she had a lumbar fusion.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has no smoking or alcohol.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the back: The patient has a normal gait. She does have some stiffness with rotation. She doesn't have any pain or numbness or tingling down her legs. She is able to feel me touching down here. She is able to lift her leg without any pain. She is able to feel me lifting her leg. She doesn't have any pain or numbness or tingling down her legs.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine is reviewed and there is no evidence of compression fractures. It does show a lumbar fusion at T4.

ASSESSMENT

1. Lumbar strain.
2. Anxiety.

PLAN

The patient will continue the motrin at 800 mg, three times a day with food. I want her to use some ice and heat, alternating on her back. I want her to walk. If her symptoms are not better or they are getting worse, I want her to come back and see me over the next week.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
