summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

High blood sugar.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she is a social drinker and a non-smoker. She lives with her family and is currently in the midst of the holiday season. She states that she has been eating a lot of sweets during this time. She notes that she has been feeling dizzy and light-headed.

MEDICATIONS

Metoprolol 25 mg twice a day
Protonix 40 mg once a day

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports feeling dizzy and light-headed.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath. Reports heart racing when anxious.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Endorses history of reflux. Reports that she has been doing well on protonix 40 mg once a day.
• Musculoskeletal: Endorses history of osteoarthritis.
• Neurological: Denies headaches or muscle aches.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea.
• Genitourinary: Denies burning with urination.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• General: In no distress.
• Neck: Thyroid is slightly enlarged, non-tender.
• Cardiovascular: Heart is in irregular rate and rhythm.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear.
• Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema in the lower extremities.

RESULTS

• Blood Glucose: 200 mg/dL
• Hemoglobin A1C: 7.8

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Diabetes Mellitus.
• Type II diabetes. The patient reports a high blood sugar. Her blood glucose is elevated and her hemoglobin A1C is elevated. We will order a complete metabolic panel to evaluate her blood sugar levels and order autoimmune labs to determine if she has an autoimmune disorder. We will start her on metformin 500 mg twice a day to help keep her blood sugars down.
• Atrial Fibrillation.
• Status post atrial fibrillation. The patient is in a good rate. We will continue her on metoprolol 25 mg twice a day.
• Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease.
• Status post gastroesophageal reflux disease. The patient reports she has been doing well on protonix 40 mg once a day. We will continue her on this medication.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will continue to avoid foods high in sugar. The nurse will come in and teach her how to use a glucometer and how to do blood sugar testing. She will start metformin 500 mg twice a day. If her autoimmune labs come back showing that she has an autoimmune disorder, we will start her on insulin. }}<|eot_id|>
