summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He was first seen in May 2020 with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus and he underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed.

The patient reports that he has been doing much better since his surgery. His walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room. However, he still has times when he needs assistance. His bladder control has improved, but has leveled out. He is now wearing diapers. He denies any sores from wearing the diaper, or any decreased or painful urination. He also denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is.

PAST HISTORY

Medical History: Adult hydrocephalus.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Shunt surgery on August 1, 2020.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient lives with his daughter, Theresa. She is the one who helps him keep things straight.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports that his sister, Hannah, summarized his condition well. She stated that he can have a normal conversation with him again.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: No clubbing, cyanosis, or clubbing. No jaundice. No lymphadenopathy. No rash.
Gastrointestinal: Bowel sounds present. No masses, tenderness, or guarding.
Genitourinary: No masses, tenderness, or guarding. No blood in the urine.
Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination slow from finger to nose.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.

RESULTS

CT of the head on 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus. Gait impairment. Urinary incontinence and urgency. Cognitive impairment.

Patient was reviewed and counselled regarding his recent CT scan. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. However, he is not experiencing any symptoms at this time and I do not believe it is causing any symptoms. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in and change it.

I do not believe his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver's test. However, he could definitely improve over the next 9-12 months and at that point we can continue to discuss this. He could also enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation. He will need to continue to use a cane for ambulation.

Patient will continue to follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
