Right upper quadrant abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Jackson is a pleasant 62-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The patient reports that this has been going on for several weeks. He denies fevers, chills, and weight loss. He has had nausea and vomiting. He has been avoiding fatty foods. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have gotten better with his diet. He also notes that he is still having pain. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.

The patient endorses the pain on the right side of his belly, underneath his ribs. It feels like a squeezing severe pain. It comes and goes. When it comes it is severe and he gets nauseated and sick to his stomach. He rates the pain as an 8 out of 10. The pain is usually worse after meals, but it can also come on a couple of hours after meals.

The patient reports that he has been able to continue with his exercise and photography.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, and weight loss.
• Gastrointestinal: Endorses nausea and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.
• Endocrine: Reports type 2 diabetes.
• Musculoskeletal: Endorses pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs.
• Abdomen: Soft, nontender abdomen. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy's sign. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

• Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Cholecystitis with gallstones.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right upper quadrant abdominal pain. He denies fevers, chills, and weight loss. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have gotten better with his diet. He has a family history of gallbladder disease. His abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder and mild thickening of the gallbladder. Given the persistent symptoms and the presence of gallstones, I recommend surgical intervention. We will proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This is a minimally invasive procedure that will involve making a few small incisions and inserting a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder through one of those small incisions. The procedure will take about an hour to an hour and a half. He will spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He will need to avoid high-fat foods after surgery. We will continue to monitor his type 2 diabetes. He will follow up with his endocrinologist or primary care provider.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his endocrinologist or primary care provider. }}<|eot_id|>
