summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Rodriguez is a pleasant 34-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain.

The onset of her knee pain started about 6 months ago. The patient denies any specific injury or trauma that caused the pain. She denies any trauma, injury or fall. She states that she has had the pain for 6 months. She states that it is a deep, aching pain that is behind her kneecaps. She denies any swelling or redness. She states that she can hear creaking in her knees. She denies any fever or chills. She states that she has tried to use ice and ibuprofen for her pain. She denies any past medical history of knee injuries. She denies any past surgeries. She denies any history of bone or joint problems.

The patient is a runner and states that she has decreased her running distance. She states that she can only run half a mile. She denies any pain with walking. She states that her pain is worse with standing. She states that she is unable to run. She denies any pain with stairs. She denies any pain with squatting.

The patient denies any numbness or tingling. She denies any tingling or numbness in her legs. She denies any muscle weakness. She denies any muscle weakness. She denies any loss of coordination.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient is married with two children. She works from home. She is a runner.

MEDICATIONS

The patient is currently taking ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Neurological: Denies any numbness or tingling. Denies any tingling or numbness in her legs. Denies any muscle weakness. Denies any muscle weakness. Denies any loss of coordination. Respiratory: Denies any shortness of breath.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the Heart: Regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs.

Examination of the Musculoskeletal: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5. There is tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. There is positive patellar grind test. She is able to squat but it hurts. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the bilateral knees: No fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bilateral knee pain.

Based on the patient's history and physical exam findings, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see that causes knee pain especially in active young people. This condition has to do with the way her kneecap which is her patella how that moves along the groove of her thigh her thigh bone which is the femur.

The patient may continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises and this will help increase her lower extremity strength her mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have.

I want to continue to assess her progress with each visit. I will be following up with her soon. I want her to take it easy for now from running. }}<|eot_id|>
