summary is: {{ History of present illness: 
The patient is a 52-year-old female who is in atrial fibrillation, with a history of high blood sugar. She has been having high blood sugar levels for a few weeks and has been experiencing lightheadedness, dizziness, and seeing spots. She has been eating a lot of sweets lately and has been having difficulty controlling her blood sugar levels.

Past medical history: 
The patient has a history of osteoarthritis and atrial fibrillation. She is also taking metoprolol 25mg twice a day for her atrial fibrillation.

Social history: 
The patient is living with her family and is finding it difficult to control her diet and blood sugar levels.

Physical examination: 
The patient appears in no distress, has a normal blood pressure and pulse, and has a normal lung exam. Her abdomen is soft and non-tender. Her thyroid is slightly enlarged but is non-tender. Her heart is in irregular rhythm and her lungs are clear.

Impression: 
The patient has a diagnosis of diabetes. She is likely to have type ii diabetes but we will need to wait for the results of the blood tests to confirm this. We will start her on metformin 500mg twice a day to help control her blood sugar levels. We will also need to send some autoimmune labs to rule out type i diabetes.

Plan: 
The patient will be started on metformin 500mg twice a day. We will also send some autoimmune labs to rule out type i diabetes. The patient will need to avoid foods high in sugar and will need to start doing blood sugar testing. She will also need to follow up with her doctor in a week to check her blood sugar levels. }}<|eot_id|>
