summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
The patient is a 67-year-old male who presented with a shoulder injury after playing tackle football. He reported pain on the back of his right shoulder that started 4 days ago. He is right-handed and has no numbness or tingling in his fingers. He is having difficulty lifting his arm above his head and has pain on the back of his shoulder with abduction and adduction. He has also had pain on the back of his shoulder with supination and pronation of the right arm. 

Past Medical History: 
He has had an appendectomy 3-4 years ago. 

Physical Examination: 
He has pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. He has decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees only. He has pain with supination and pronation of the right arm and palpable radial radial artery pulse. 

Impression: 
The patient has an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. 

Plan: 
He was advised to wear a sling for now. He was prescribed meloxicam 15mg once a day and advised to take it for 2 weeks. He was also advised to follow up with the doctor in 2 weeks. He was advised to avoid playing tackle football for a while and to avoid heavy lifting. He was referred to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. He was advised to avoid playing flag football in the future as well. }}<|eot_id|>
