summary is: {{ History of present illness: 
The patient is a 50-year-old male who has been experiencing lower back pain for about 4 months. He has been having pain in his lower back, which has been getting better and worse. He has had pain in his lower back when he is sitting, moving, and lying down. He has had numbness and tingling in his right foot, which has resolved. He has also had difficulty lifting heavy objects and has been experiencing sharp pain in his lower back when rolling over in his sleep. 
Past medical history: 
The patient has had a total knee replacement 2 years ago.
Social history: 
The patient has been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for his back pain. He has also tried heat and ice, but they have not helped. He has been having pain for about 4 months, and he has not had any imaging done. He has been trying to avoid going to the doctor because he is concerned about the cost. 
Physical examination: 
The patient has a normal temperature, blood pressure, and oxygen saturation. His heart rate is 68. He has some bony protuberance of the C5-C6. He has no pain to palpation of the lumbar spine, but he has decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. He has a positive straight leg raise and some spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. His lower extremities strength is equal bilaterally. 
Assessment: 
