summary is: {{ History of present illness: 
Dennis is a 57-year-old male who has been experiencing shortness of breath for a couple of weeks. He has a history of diabetes and high blood pressure and asthma. He wakes up with shortness of breath and feels short of breath again when he goes back home. He has been coughing up blood for about three weeks. He has been coughing up a lot of blood, about three to four cups a day. He has not been throwing up any blood and has not had any bleeding anywhere else. He has not had any bruising or bleeding from his teeth or gums. He has not had any recent travel history. He has had a knee problem, but there is no swelling and it has been going on for a while. He has not been to the hospital or the emergency room for his coughing and coughing up blood. He has been trying to get an appointment in the office but was unable to get one. 

Physical examination: 
His oxygen level in the clinic is 94% on pulse oxygenation. His pulse is regular and his blood pressure is 120/70. He has no jugular venous distension. He has no carotid bruits. His lung exam shows some rhonchi and rails on the right side. He has a diminished breath sound on the right side. His heart exam shows a normal rate and rhythm with a two plus ejection murmur. His belly exam is soft and his extremity exam shows one plus non-pitting edema on both lower extremities. He has no calf tenderness and no positive homan sign. 

Impression: 
He has an infection in his lungs that is causing his shortness of breath. He needs to go to the emergency department for a chest X-ray and a CAT scan to evaluate why he is having this shortness of breath and why he is coughing up blood. He will need to have some blood testing done. 

Plan: 
He will be referred to the emergency department to get a chest X-ray and a CAT scan. He will need to go to the emergency department to get some blood testing done to evaluate his shortness of breath and coughing up blood. He will need to hold off on his norvasc for now and will need to come back in three weeks to discuss his diabetes and treatment plan. He will need to hold off on his norvasc for now and will need to come back in three weeks to discuss his diabetes and treatment plan. He will need to come back in three weeks to discuss his diabetes and treatment plan. He will need to come back in three weeks to discuss his diabetes and treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
