summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
The patient is a 36-year-old male who presents with a 1-day history of right ankle pain and swelling after falling on the ice. He states that he slipped on the ice while taking out the trash and landed on the ground. He was helped up by a friend and has been keeping the ankle elevated and icing it since then. He has been able to put a little bit of weight on it but is still limping.

Past Medical History: The patient has a history of playing soccer and has had multiple injuries in the past, but does not recall injuring this ankle specifically.

Past Surgical History: None
Medications: Ibuprofen

Physical Examination:
- The patient has ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus
- There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue
- There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress
- There is no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot
- Neurovascular exam of the right foot is intact, with capillary refill less than 3 seconds and strong dorsalis pedis pulse

Impression: Right ankle sprain
Plan:
- Continue to elevate the ankle and ice it
- Use crutches for the next 1-2 days
- Start walking on it as tolerated after 1-2 days
- Follow up in 2 weeks
- Take nsaids as needed for pain
- Consider an aircast to stabilize the ankle
- Review the results of the ankle x-ray, which showed no fracture
- Review the ankle x-ray again in 2 weeks to assess for any signs of osteomyelitis or septic arthritis. }}<|eot_id|>
